<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>The seven sacraments in life</h4>
<p>Do not present the seven sacraments as a list to memorize. Present them as stages of Christian life: birth (Baptism), growth (Confirmation), nourishment (Eucharist), healing (Penance, Anointing), mission (Holy Orders, Matrimony).</p>

<h4>Ex opere operato</h4>
<p>This is a key concept but can sound technical. Explain it with an example: a faucet connected to the main water pipe &mdash; the water comes from the source (Christ), not from the faucet (minister).</p>

<h4>Sacramental character</h4>
<p>Help the group to understand that the character is like a spiritual tattoo. It cannot be erased, it cannot be repeated. Being baptized is not something that <em>&laquo;happened to us&raquo;</em> when we were children; it is an eternal reality.</p>

<h4>The Passover as a type</h4>
<p>It is important to show that the Bible is not a collection of unrelated books, but a unified story. The Passover of Israel prefigures the Passover of Christ. The blood of the lamb, the unleavened bread, the liberation &mdash; all find their fulfillment in Jesus.</p>

<h4>The sacraments are not magical</h4>
<p>Emphasize that although the sacraments work <em>ex opere operato</em>, they require disposition. Grace is always offered, but the fruit depends on our response. It is like rain: it falls on everyone, but the soil must be prepared to receive it.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use the image of seven gifts. Each sacrament is a special gift from Jesus. Activities: color the seven sacraments, memorize their names with a song, draw the Passover (little lamb, cross, light).</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect the sacraments with important milestones in their lives: which sacraments have they received? Which would they like to receive? Talk about Matrimony and Holy Orders as vocations. Ask: <em>&laquo;What does it mean to you to have been marked by God?&raquo;</em></p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults have not received all the sacraments or received them without preparation. Help them rediscover the value of each one. Invite them to consider Confirmation or Reconciliation if they have not received them recently.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the theology of character as participation in Christ's priesthood. Relate it to Church Tradition (St. Thomas Aquinas, Council of Trent). Discuss how character configures Christian identity in a world that rejects permanent identities.</p>

</section>

