<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4><em>&laquo;If the sacraments work ex opere operato, what is faith for?&raquo;</em></h4>
<p>Faith does not cause the grace of the sacrament, but it is necessary to receive it fruitfully. The sacrament gives grace as long as there is no obstacle (mortal sin), but the fruit &mdash;growth in holiness&mdash; depends on the disposition of the recipient.</p>

<h4><em>&laquo;Why do I have to confess to a priest? Can't I confess directly to God?&raquo;</em></h4>
<p>God always hears our prayer. But Christ instituted the sacrament of Penance as a visible sign of his forgiveness. The priest acts <em>in persona Christi</em>: when he absolves, it is Christ who absolves. Moreover, confessing aloud has a psychological and spiritual effect: it helps us be honest with ourselves.</p>

<h4><em>&laquo;Can I receive Communion if I'm not married in the Church?&raquo;</em></h4>
<p>Only if you are living in a situation that the Church considers irregular does Communion express a full communion that does not exist. But you can always come to Mass, make a spiritual communion, and seek to regularize your situation with the help of a priest.</p>

<h4><em>&laquo;What happens if I receive a sacrament without faith or in sin?&raquo;</em></h4>
<p>If you receive a sacrament in a state of mortal sin (such as Communion), you commit a sacrilege. But the sacrament remains valid. It is like receiving a precious gift and throwing it in the trash: the gift is still valuable, but it does not benefit you.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus, source of all grace,<br />
thank you for the sacraments, channels of your love.<br />
Help me to convey to those who listen to me<br />
that in every sacrament you act, you heal, you nourish.<br />
May I never grow accustomed to the wonder of your grace,<br />
may I always approach the sacraments with faith and reverence.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

